  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most roof leaks in Temple and the rest of Bell County don't start in the middle of a field of shingles. They start at the edges, seams, and penetrations, the places where the roof has to change direction or make room for a pipe, a chimney, or a wall. Knowing the common causes of roof leaks helps you describe the problem accurately, avoid paying for the wrong fix, and decide how urgent it really is. This guide walks through what we see most often on Central Texas homes, why our climate makes certain failures more likely, and what you can do safely from inside the house while you wait for help.</p>

<div class="callout callout--warn"><strong>Safety first:</strong> Never go up on the roof to look for a leak, especially when it's wet, windy, or hot. Shingles get slick in rain and soft in summer heat, and most leak sources aren't visible from the surface anyway. Everything in this article can be done from the ground, from inside the house, or from a safely accessible attic.</div>

<h2>Why Central Texas Roofs Leak the Way They Do</h2>
<p>Our weather is hard on roofing materials in a specific pattern. Spring brings hail and straight-line winds, usually somewhere between March and June, with a second round of storms in the fall. Summers routinely push into the upper 90s and past 100°F, and the sun is intense for months at a time. Occasional hard freezes, like the February 2021 winter storm, add a different kind of stress. Then there are the heavy downpours that can dump a lot of water in a short time.</p>
<p>That combination means rubber and sealant age faster than you might expect, metal flashing moves as it heats and cools, and drainage gets tested hard a few times a year.</p>

<h2>The Most Common Causes of Roof Leaks We Find</h2>
<p>Below are the usual suspects, roughly in the order we tend to check them when a homeowner calls about a fresh stain.</p>

<h3>1. Flashing Failures</h3>
<p>Flashing is the metal that seals transitions: where a roof meets a wall, around a chimney, along a dormer, or at a step in the roofline. It's designed to shed water, not hold it back under pressure, so it depends on correct overlap and placement. Common problems include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Missing or short step flashing</strong> along sidewalls, often from an earlier repair or a hurried install.</li>
  <li><strong>Counter flashing pulled loose</strong> from brick or stucco as mortar joints crumble.</li>
  <li><strong>Sealant used as a substitute</strong> for proper flashing. Caulk can look fine for a year or two, then crack and let water straight in.</li>
  <li><strong>Rusted or bent flashing</strong> on older homes, particularly in central Temple neighborhoods where some roofs have been re-shingled more than once over original metal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Flashing leaks often show up on ceilings or walls next to an exterior wall, a fireplace, or a second-story addition, and they may only appear when rain comes from a certain direction.</p>

<h3>2. Pipe Boots Cracked by UV</h3>
<p>Every plumbing vent that comes through your roof is sealed with a pipe boot, typically a rubber or neoprene collar on a metal or plastic base. In our sun, that rubber dries out and splits, sometimes years before the shingles around it are worn. A cracked boot lets water run down the outside of the pipe and into the attic, where it can drip onto insulation and drywall below.</p>
<p>Pipe boot leaks are among the most common causes of roof leaks in homes that are otherwise in decent shape. The good news is that a boot replacement is usually a modest <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a>, provided the decking around it hasn't been rotting for a long time.</p>

<h3>3. Valleys</h3>
<p>A valley is where two roof slopes meet and form a channel. It carries more water than any other part of the roof, so any weakness gets exposed quickly. Valleys leak when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Shingles are cut too short or the underlayment beneath them was skimped on.</li>
  <li>Leaves, pine needles, or debris build up and dam the water, forcing it sideways under the shingles.</li>
  <li>Hail or foot traffic has damaged the shingles in the valley, where wear is concentrated.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Nail Pops and Exposed Fasteners</h3>
<p>As decking expands and contracts through hot days and cooler nights, roofing nails can back out and push up against the shingle above. Eventually the shingle tears or lifts, leaving a small hole right over the nail. Exposed fasteners on ridge caps, vents, and flashing also need sealing, and that sealant won't last forever in our UV. These leaks tend to be small, steady drips that leave a round stain or a single wet spot on the ceiling.</p>

<h3>5. Storm Damage</h3>
<p>Hail can fracture shingles and knock off the granules that protect the asphalt, and strong winds can crease or lift shingles so their seal strip no longer holds. Neither always causes an immediate leak. Often the damage lets water in weeks or months later, once the exposed mat has been baked and soaked a few times. If a leak appears after a spring storm, it's worth having the whole roof looked at rather than just patching one spot. Our <a href="/services/storm-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">storm damage roof repair</a> page explains how we document what we find, and our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-spot-hail-damage-on-your-roof-temple-tx/">spotting hail damage</a> covers what to look for from the ground.</p>

<h3>6. Clogged Gutters and Poor Drainage</h3>
<p>Gutters that overflow during a heavy downpour can push water back under the roof edge, soak the fascia, and rot the bottom of the decking. Downspouts that dump onto a lower roof section can overload that area and cause leaks in a spot that looks perfectly fine. If you notice stains along the top of exterior walls, peeling paint on fascia boards, or water pouring over the gutter lip in a storm, drainage may be the root issue. Our <a href="/services/gutters-roof-drainage-temple-tx/">gutters and roof drainage</a> service addresses this directly.</p>

<h3>7. Skylights and Chimneys</h3>
<p>Skylights and chimneys are large openings in the roof, so they depend on several layers of flashing working together. Skylight leaks often come from failed flashing kits or deteriorated seals around the glass rather than the skylight itself. Chimneys can leak through the flashing, through a cracked crown on top, or through porous brick and mortar. A leak that only appears near the fireplace after long, soaking rains often points to the chimney itself rather than the roofing around it.</p>

<h3>8. Wind-Driven Rain</h3>
<p>Some leaks only show up when storms blow in hard from one direction. Wind can push water uphill under shingles, through ridge vents, past gable vents, and around poorly sealed flashing. If your stain appears only during certain storms and stays dry through ordinary rain, wind-driven water is a strong possibility. Note the wind direction when it happens; it's a useful clue for whoever inspects the roof. Our <a href="/services/wind-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">wind damage roof repair</a> page explains how lifted shingles and vents are handled.</p>

<!--cta-->

<h2>Condensation vs. a Real Roof Leak</h2>
<p>Not every wet spot in the attic means water is getting through the roof. Condensation forms when warm, moist air meets a cold surface. In Central Texas this happens most often in winter, when warm air from the house rises into a cold attic, and around HVAC equipment and ductwork in the attic during humid summer weather.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Clue</th><th>Points toward condensation</th><th>Points toward a roof leak</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Timing</td><td>Appears on cold mornings or humid days with no rain</td><td>Appears during or shortly after rain</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pattern</td><td>Moisture spread across many nail tips or sheathing</td><td>One concentrated spot or a single drip trail</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Location</td><td>Near ducts, the AC unit, or bathroom exhaust fans</td><td>Near pipes, chimneys, valleys, or walls</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Insulation</td><td>Damp in broad areas</td><td>Matted or stained in one place</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Bathroom fans that vent into the attic instead of outside, sweating AC lines, and poor attic ventilation are common condensation sources. Our article on <a href="/blog/texas-summer-heat-roof-attic-ventilation/">summer heat and attic ventilation</a> goes deeper into how airflow affects moisture.</p>

<h2>How to Trace a Leak Safely From Inside</h2>
<p>Water rarely drips straight down. It can run along a rafter or pipe for several feet before it falls, so the stain on your ceiling is a clue rather than a map. Here's a safe way to narrow things down:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Mark and photograph the stain.</strong> Trace its outline lightly in pencil and take a dated photo. If it grows after the next rain, you'll know the leak is active.</li>
  <li><strong>Check the attic only if it's safe.</strong> Use a sturdy ladder, a good flashlight, and step only on joists or a walkway. Avoid the attic during summer afternoons when temperatures up there can become dangerous.</li>
  <li><strong>Look uphill from the stain.</strong> Start at the spot above the stain and follow the roof slope upward. Look for dark streaks on the decking, rusty nail tips, damp insulation, or daylight around a pipe.</li>
  <li><strong>Check around penetrations.</strong> Plumbing vents, exhaust fans, and chimney chases are the first places to look.</li>
  <li><strong>Note the weather.</strong> Write down whether the leak shows up in every rain, only heavy rain, or only wind-driven storms.</li>
  <li><strong>Look from the ground outside.</strong> Binoculars help you spot missing shingles, a lifted pipe boot, debris in a valley, or overflowing gutters.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Bring those notes and photos when you call a roofer. A clear history like "only during storms from the south, stain near the bathroom vent" can save time and help pinpoint the repair.</div>

<h2>Temporary Steps While You Wait for a Repair</h2>
<p>Once you know water is coming in, a few simple steps can limit the damage inside:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Catch the water.</strong> Put a bucket or tote under the drip, with a towel inside to reduce splashing.</li>
  <li><strong>Relieve a bulging ceiling.</strong> If drywall is sagging with trapped water, a small hole poked at the lowest point (with a bucket ready) can prevent a larger collapse. Stand to the side and keep people and pets clear.</li>
  <li><strong>Protect belongings.</strong> Move furniture and electronics, and cover what you can't move with plastic.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch for electrical hazards.</strong> If water is near a light fixture or outlet, turn off that circuit at the breaker and keep everyone away.</li>
  <li><strong>Leave tarping to professionals.</strong> A properly secured tarp can prevent more damage, but it requires working on the roof. If you need one, our <a href="/services/emergency-roof-leak-repair-temple-tx/">emergency roof leak repair</a> team can help.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the leak followed a storm, take photos of the interior damage before you clean up. Our <a href="/tools/storm-damage-checklist/">storm damage checklist</a> walks through what to record, and the <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claim-assistance-temple-tx/">insurance claim assistance</a> page explains how a roofer can document damage for your claim (we're roofers, not public adjusters, so the claim itself stays between you and your insurer).</p>

<h2>Repair or Something Bigger?</h2>
<p>Most single-point leaks, such as a pipe boot, a section of flashing, or a few nail pops, can be fixed without replacing the roof. Repeated leaks in different places, widespread granule loss, curling shingles, or soft decking are signs the roof may be near the end of its life. Our article on <a href="/blog/roof-repair-vs-replacement-temple/">roof repair vs. replacement</a> walks through how to weigh the two, and the <a href="/tools/roof-replacement-cost-calculator/">roof replacement cost calculator</a> gives a rough starting point if replacement is on the table.</p>

<h2>Next Steps for a Dry Ceiling</h2>
<p>Leaks get more expensive the longer they run, because the water you see is only part of what's soaking into decking, insulation, and framing. Keep notes and photos, and have the leak traced to its actual source rather than patching the stain. Keeping up with gutters and seasonal checks helps prevent many of these problems in the first place; our <a href="/blog/roof-maintenance-checklist-temple-homeowners/">roof maintenance checklist</a> lays out what to look at and when. When you're ready, <a href="/free-roof-inspection/">schedule a free roof inspection</a> with Temple Roofers and we'll trace the leak, photograph what we find, and give you a written estimate for the repair.</p>
